  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. Left-right (x) is 3 times steeper, so overshoot shows there first: x swings grow once learning rate passes 2/3, so runs at 1 diverge.",
    "Noise: each slope component gets random noise with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly zero at 64. Values come from a fixed seed.",
    "Both arrows are steps (learning rate × slope estimate), drawn 3 times their true length on one scale. Mini-batch step is where the ball moves next; Full-data step is against the true slope. Hidden when the run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's steep edge (diverged). Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 step landings (41 points); the kept ghost path likewise.",
    "Runs advance five steps per second at speed 1. Changing a setting mid-run affects the next steps only.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average; small batches can raise it on single steps."
  ]
}